-- Q1: medium / L2 — Encumbered property contribution triggering gain under 752/731
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$Whitaker contributes land with a basis of $40,000 and FMV of $250,000 to a new 50-50 partnership. The land is subject to a $120,000 nonrecourse mortgage that the partnership assumes. After the contribution, Whitaker's share of the assumed liability is 50% ($60,000). What are the tax consequences to Whitaker?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No gain recognized; outside basis is $40,000", "Gain of $20,000 recognized; outside basis is $0", "Gain of $60,000 recognized; outside basis is $0", "No gain recognized; outside basis is $100,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 752(b), the partnership's assumption of Whitaker's $120,000 liability is a deemed distribution. Whitaker is relieved of $120,000 but picks up 50% ($60,000) under Section 752(a). Net deemed distribution is $60,000 ($120,000 - $60,000). Under Section 731(a), gain is recognized to the extent the deemed distribution ($60,000) exceeds basis ($40,000) — yielding $20,000 gain. Basis is reduced to $0. Wrong (A): The net liability relief exceeds basis, so gain must be recognized under Section 731(a). Wrong (C): Gain equals the excess of net deemed distribution ($60,000) over basis ($40,000), not the full deemed distribution. Wrong (D): This ignores the liability relief rules entirely.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — S corp per-share per-day allocation on mid-year stock transfer
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$Jennings owns 100% of Apex S Corp for the first 219 days of the year, then sells all shares to Barlow on August 8 (day 220). Apex reports $365,000 of ordinary income for the full year. No Section 1377(a)(2) election is made. How much income is allocated to Barlow for basis purposes?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$365,000 because Barlow owns 100% at year-end", "$146,000 based on 146/365 days of ownership", "$182,500 split equally between the two shareholders", "$219,000 based on Jennings's ownership period"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 1377(a)(1), absent an election to close the books, S corporation items are allocated on a per-share, per-day basis. Barlow owns shares for 146 days (August 8 through December 31). Barlow's allocation is $365,000 x (146/365) = $146,000, which increases Barlow's stock basis. Wrong (A): Year-end ownership does not determine allocation; the per-share per-day rule applies. Wrong (C): An equal split ignores the per-share per-day requirement. Wrong (D): $219,000 is Jennings's allocation, not Barlow's.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: hard / L4 — Section 737 pre-contribution gain recognition on distribution
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$Osgood contributed appreciated equipment (basis $30,000, FMV $130,000) to a partnership three years ago. The partnership now distributes other property worth $200,000 (basis $80,000) to Osgood in a current distribution. Osgood's outside basis before the distribution is $90,000. Under Section 737, the pre-contribution gain is $100,000 ($130,000 FMV minus $30,000 basis). The net pre-contribution gain is $100,000 and the excess distribution is $110,000 ($200,000 FMV minus $90,000 outside basis). Evaluate how much gain Osgood must recognize.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$110,000 gain equal to the excess of distributed property FMV over outside basis", "$100,000 gain limited to the pre-contribution gain in the contributed equipment", "$200,000 gain equal to the FMV of distributed property", "$0 gain because current distributions are generally tax-free"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 737(a), when a partner receives a distribution of property other than the property originally contributed, the partner recognizes gain equal to the lesser of: (1) the excess of distributed property FMV over outside basis ($200,000 - $90,000 = $110,000), or (2) the net pre-contribution gain ($100,000). The lesser amount is $100,000. This prevents partners from extracting value without recognizing built-in gain. Wrong (A): Section 737 caps gain at the pre-contribution gain, not the full excess. Wrong (C): Full FMV gain ignores the Section 737 limitation framework entirely. Wrong (D): Section 737 creates an exception to the general tax-free treatment of current distributions when pre-contribution gain exists.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q4: medium / L3 — Marketable securities treated as cash under Section 731(c)
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$Pratt receives a current distribution of marketable securities with a FMV of $120,000 and an inside basis of $45,000 from her partnership. Pratt's outside basis before the distribution is $80,000. Under Section 731(c), marketable securities are treated as cash for distribution purposes. Determine whether Pratt recognizes gain and what her basis in the securities is.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Gain of $40,000; basis in securities is $80,000", "No gain; basis in securities is $45,000 (carryover)", "No gain; basis in securities is $80,000 (substituted)", "Gain of $75,000; basis in securities is $120,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 731(c), marketable securities distributed by a partnership are treated as money. When the FMV ($120,000) exceeds the partner's outside basis ($80,000), gain of $40,000 is recognized under Section 731(a)(1). The basis in the securities equals the partner's outside basis ($80,000) reduced to $0 for the deemed cash, but under Section 731(c)(3)(B), the basis is increased by the gain recognized, resulting in a basis of $80,000. Wrong (B): Carryover basis ignores the Section 731(c) cash treatment rule for marketable securities. Wrong (C): While $80,000 is the correct basis, the gain must also be recognized. Wrong (D): Gain is the excess of FMV over outside basis ($40,000), not FMV minus inside basis.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q5: medium / L3 — Section 732(d) election for transferee partner distributions
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$Osborne purchased a 25% partnership interest for $400,000 when the partnership had no Section 754 election. The partnership's total inside basis was $1,200,000 (Osborne's 25% share = $300,000). Two years later, the partnership distributes an asset with an inside basis of $50,000 and FMV of $150,000 to Osborne. Osborne makes a Section 732(d) election. Analyze how the election affects Osborne's basis in the distributed asset.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Osborne takes a $50,000 carryover basis in the asset", "Osborne takes a $150,000 FMV basis in the asset", "Osborne adjusts the asset's basis as if a Section 754 election had been in effect at purchase, resulting in a stepped-up basis", "The Section 732(d) election applies only to liquidating distributions"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 732(d), a transferee partner who acquired an interest by purchase (and no Section 754 election was in effect) may elect to treat the partnership assets as if a Section 743(b) adjustment had been made. This allows Osborne to step up the distributed asset's basis to reflect what the 743(b) adjustment would have been, resulting in a higher basis than the $50,000 carryover. Wrong (A): The Section 732(d) election specifically allows the transferee to avoid the carryover basis result. Wrong (B): FMV basis is not the result; the election applies a hypothetical 743(b) adjustment. Wrong (D): Section 732(d) applies to current distributions as well, not only liquidating ones.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q6: hard / L4 — Disguised sale under Section 707(a)(2)(B) and basis consequences
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$Wolfe contributes property with a basis of $60,000 and FMV of $500,000 to a new partnership. Within 30 days, the partnership distributes $400,000 cash to Wolfe. Under the Section 707(a)(2)(B) disguised sale rules and the two-year presumption in Treas. Reg. 1.707-3(c), evaluate the tax consequences to Wolfe assuming the presumption is not rebutted.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No gain recognized; the contribution and distribution are independent events under Section 721 and 731", "Wolfe recognizes $340,000 of gain because the transaction is treated as a sale of 80% of the property ($400,000/$500,000) for $400,000", "Wolfe recognizes $440,000 of gain equal to the full FMV minus basis", "Wolfe recognizes $400,000 of gain equal to the cash distribution received"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 707(a)(2)(B) and Treas. Reg. 1.707-3(c), a contribution followed by a distribution within two years creates a presumption of a disguised sale. The portion treated as sold equals $400,000/$500,000 = 80%. The amount realized is $400,000, and basis allocated to the sold portion is 80% x $60,000 = $48,000, yielding $352,000 of gain. The remaining 20% is a valid contribution with $12,000 basis. Wrong (A): The disguised sale rules override the separate treatment of contributions and distributions. Wrong (C): Gain is limited to the disguised sale portion, not the full FMV minus basis. Wrong (D): Gain equals amount realized minus allocable basis, not the full distribution.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q7: medium / L3 — Partnership charitable contribution and outside basis
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$Colton is a 30% partner with an outside basis of $200,000. The partnership donates appreciated stock (basis $50,000, FMV $180,000) to a qualified charity. Colton's 30% share of the charitable contribution deduction is $54,000 (based on FMV). Determine the effect on Colton's outside basis.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Basis decreases by $54,000 (Colton's share of the FMV deduction) to $146,000", "Basis decreases by $15,000 (Colton's 30% share of the property's $50,000 basis) to $185,000", "Basis decreases by $180,000 (the full FMV of the donated property) to $20,000", "Basis is unchanged because charitable contributions do not affect outside basis"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 705(a)(2)(B), a partner's outside basis is decreased by the partner's share of the partnership's basis in the contributed property, not by the FMV or the charitable deduction amount. Colton's share is 30% of $50,000 = $15,000, reducing basis from $200,000 to $185,000. The deduction amount ($54,000 based on FMV) is a separately stated item but does not determine the basis reduction. Wrong (A): The FMV-based deduction reduces taxable income but does not determine the basis adjustment. Wrong (C): Only the partner's share of basis is used, not the full FMV. Wrong (D): Charitable contributions do reduce outside basis — by the partner's share of the donated property's basis.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — Family attribution rules under Section 318 affecting S corp eligibility
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$Rivera owns 60 shares of Apex Corp, an S corporation with 100 total shares. Rivera's adult daughter, Tate, owns the remaining 40 shares. Rivera gifts 25 shares to an irrevocable trust for the benefit of Tate's minor child. Under Section 318 family attribution rules, how many shareholders does Apex Corp have for purposes of the 100-shareholder limit under Section 1361?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["One shareholder, because all shares are attributed to Rivera as the family patriarch", "Three shareholders: Rivera, Tate, and the trust", "Two shareholders: Rivera and Tate, because family members count as one shareholder under Section 1361(c)(1)", "The trust disqualifies Apex from S corporation status entirely"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 1361(b)(1)(A), S corporations may have no more than 100 shareholders. Rivera, Tate, and the trust are each counted separately. While Section 1361(c)(1) allows family members to elect to be treated as one shareholder for the 100-shareholder limit, this applies only to members of a family within six generations of a common ancestor who affirmatively elect. Absent such an election, each is a separate shareholder. The trust qualifies as a permitted S corporation shareholder under Section 1361(c)(2). Wrong (A): Section 318 attribution rules apply for stock ownership determination, not for collapsing shareholder count absent an election. Wrong (C): The family-as-one-shareholder rule under Section 1361(c)(1) requires an affirmative election; it is not automatic. Wrong (D): Trusts for the benefit of family members qualify as S corporation shareholders under Section 1361(c)(2)(A).$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Section 108 debt discharge exclusion effect on S corp basis
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$Marshall is the sole shareholder of Dalton Inc., an S corporation. Dalton has $200,000 of debt discharged in a Title 11 bankruptcy proceeding. Under Section 108(a)(1)(A), the discharge is excluded from income. Marshall's stock basis before the discharge is $50,000. How does the excluded discharge affect Marshall's stock basis?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Stock basis increases by $200,000 to $250,000", "Stock basis is unchanged at $50,000 because excluded income does not flow through", "Stock basis decreases to $0 because tax attributes are reduced under Section 108(b)", "Stock basis increases by $200,000, then decreases by $200,000 under attribute reduction, netting to $50,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 108(d)(7)(A), debt discharge excluded under Section 108(a) at the S corporation level is applied at the corporate level, not passed through to the shareholder. The exclusion does not increase the shareholder's stock basis because it is not included in the S corporation's income. Tax attribute reduction under Section 108(b) occurs at the corporate level. Wrong (A): Excluded discharge income does not flow through to increase shareholder basis. Wrong (C): Attribute reduction occurs at the corporate level; the shareholder's stock basis is not directly reduced. Wrong (D): The offset description is incorrect; the exclusion simply does not flow through to the shareholder.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: easy / L1 — What separately stated items are for basis purposes
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$Why must certain S corporation income and deduction items be "separately stated" rather than combined into nonseparately computed income?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["To increase the total income allocated to shareholders", "Because separately stated items may be subject to different limitations or treatment at the shareholder level", "To reduce the corporation's tax liability before allocation", "Because the IRS requires all items to be separately stated without exception"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 1366(a)(1)(A), items whose character or amount could affect a shareholder's individual tax liability differently must be separately stated. For example, capital gains, charitable contributions, and tax-exempt income each have distinct treatment at the shareholder level that would be lost if netted into a single income figure. Wrong (A): Separate statement does not change total income; it preserves character for proper individual treatment. Wrong (C): S corporations are generally pass-through entities and do not pay entity-level tax on these items. Wrong (D): Only items that could have different individual-level treatment must be separately stated; routine operating items are combined.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q11: medium / L3 — S corp built-in gains tax (Section 1374) and basis interaction
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$Saxon Corp converted from C to S corporation status five years ago. It now sells an asset with a built-in gain of $200,000 (the asset had a FMV of $500,000 and basis of $300,000 at conversion). Saxon Corp pays $42,000 of built-in gains tax under Section 1374 (21% rate). The sole shareholder, Moreno, has a stock basis of $150,000. How does the sale affect Moreno's stock basis?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Increase by $200,000 (full gain passed through) to $350,000", "Increase by $158,000 (gain minus BIG tax) to $308,000", "Increase by $200,000, then decrease by $42,000 for the entity-level tax, net increase of $158,000 to $308,000", "No change because the gain is subject to entity-level tax and does not pass through"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 1366(f)(2), the full $200,000 gain passes through to Moreno, increasing basis by $200,000. However, the Section 1374 built-in gains tax of $42,000 paid at the entity level is treated as a loss under Section 1366(f)(2), reducing basis by $42,000. The net effect is a $158,000 increase, bringing Moreno's stock basis from $150,000 to $308,000. Wrong (A): This ignores the basis reduction for the BIG tax paid at the entity level. Wrong (B): While the net result is the same, the mechanism involves two separate adjustments, not a single net figure. Wrong (D): The gain does pass through to the shareholder; the entity-level tax is a separate adjustment.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Installment obligation contributed to partnership basis
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$Regan contributes an installment note receivable to a 50-50 partnership. The note has a face value of $300,000, a remaining basis of $120,000, and an FMV of $280,000. The built-in gain on the note is $160,000 ($280,000 FMV minus $120,000 basis). Regan's total outside basis is $120,000 (the carryover basis). When the partnership collects $100,000 on the note, determine how the gain is allocated under Section 704(c).$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$100,000 of ordinary income split equally between partners", "The gain portion of the $100,000 collection is allocated entirely to Regan as the contributing partner", "The gain is allocated 50-50 because both partners share profits equally", "No gain is recognized until the note is fully collected"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 704(c), the built-in gain in contributed property must be allocated to the contributing partner when the gain is realized. When the partnership collects on the installment note, the gain portion (the difference between the amount collected and the proportionate basis) is allocated to Regan. Only post-contribution gain or loss is split according to the partnership agreement. Wrong (A): Equal splitting would shift pre-contribution gain to the non-contributing partner, violating Section 704(c). Wrong (C): The profit-sharing ratio applies only to post-contribution gains, not built-in gains. Wrong (D): Gain is recognized as each installment payment is collected under the installment method.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: easy / L1 — What is the effect of nondeductible expenses on S corp basis
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$Which step in the S corporation stock basis ordering rules addresses nondeductible, non-capital expenses such as the 50% meals disallowance?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Step 1 — they increase basis like income items", "Step 2 — they are treated the same as tax-exempt income", "Step 3 — they decrease basis before distributions and losses", "Step 5 — they decrease basis last, after all other adjustments"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.1367-1(f), nondeductible non-capital expenses (such as the 50% meals disallowance, fines, or penalties) reduce stock basis at step 3, after income increases but before distributions and loss deductions. This ordering ensures these expenses reduce basis before distributions could further erode it. Wrong (A): Nondeductible expenses decrease basis, not increase it. Wrong (B): Tax-exempt income increases basis at step 2; nondeductible expenses are a separate category that decreases basis. Wrong (D): Losses and deductions decrease basis at step 5; nondeductible expenses are addressed earlier at step 3.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — Partnership technical termination abolished by TCJA
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$Before the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017, a partnership was treated as terminated under Section 708(b)(1)(B) if 50% or more of the partnership interests were sold or exchanged within a 12-month period. What is the current rule?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The technical termination rule still applies but only to partnerships with more than 100 partners", "The TCJA repealed the technical termination rule; selling 50% or more of interests no longer terminates the partnership", "The threshold was increased from 50% to 75% of interests", "Technical terminations now apply only to LLPs, not LLCs"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): The TCJA of 2017 repealed IRC Section 708(b)(1)(B), eliminating the technical termination rule for partnerships. Under current law, a partnership continues even if 50% or more of interests are sold within 12 months. The partnership's tax year, elections (including Section 754), and basis calculations continue uninterrupted. Wrong (A): The repeal applies to all partnerships regardless of size. Wrong (C): The provision was fully repealed, not modified with a different threshold. Wrong (D): The repeal applies to all entity types classified as partnerships for tax purposes.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — What triggers Section 734(b) adjustment
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$Under IRC Section 734(b), what event triggers an adjustment to the inside basis of a partnership's remaining assets?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A partner's contribution of property to the partnership", "A distribution of property or money from the partnership to a partner when a Section 754 election is in effect", "The sale of a partnership interest by one partner to another", "A partner's increase in profit-sharing percentage"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): IRC Section 734(b) provides for an adjustment to the basis of remaining partnership assets when a distribution results in a basis discrepancy, provided a Section 754 election is in effect (or the mandatory adjustment threshold under Section 734(d) is met). The adjustment preserves total basis parity within the partnership. Wrong (A): Property contributions are governed by Sections 721 and 723, not Section 734(b). Wrong (C): Sales of partnership interests trigger Section 743(b) adjustments, not Section 734(b). Wrong (D): Changes in profit-sharing percentages do not trigger inside basis adjustments under Section 734.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: easy / L1 — S corp distribution tax-free to extent of basis
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$An S corporation with no accumulated E&P distributes cash to a shareholder. To what extent is the distribution tax-free?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The entire distribution is always tax-free regardless of basis", "Tax-free to the extent of the shareholder's stock basis; excess is capital gain", "Tax-free to the extent of the corporation's retained earnings", "Tax-free only if the shareholder has held the stock for more than one year"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 1368(b), when an S corporation has no accumulated E&P, distributions are tax-free to the extent of the shareholder's stock basis. Any excess over stock basis is treated as gain from the sale of stock (typically capital gain). Wrong (A): Distributions exceeding stock basis are taxable as capital gain. Wrong (C): S corporations pass through income directly; the concept of "retained earnings" does not govern distribution taxation. Wrong (D): The holding period is irrelevant to whether the distribution is tax-free; basis determines the tax treatment.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — Partner contributing 1245 recapture property
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$Sawyer contributes equipment to a partnership under Section 721. The equipment has a basis of $25,000 and FMV of $90,000. The equipment is Section 1245 property with $65,000 of potential depreciation recapture. What is the partnership's basis in the contributed equipment and how is the recapture potential handled?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Basis of $90,000; recapture is recognized at contribution", "Basis of $25,000; recapture potential carries over to the partnership and must be allocated to Sawyer when the property is sold", "Basis of $25,000; recapture is eliminated upon contribution", "Basis of $65,000; recapture reduces the contributed basis"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 723, the partnership takes a carryover basis of $25,000 in the contributed equipment. Under Section 1245(b)(3), the contribution under Section 721 is tax-free, so no recapture occurs at contribution. However, the recapture potential carries over — when the partnership later sells the equipment, the Section 1245 ordinary income recapture must be allocated to Sawyer under Section 704(c). Wrong (A): FMV is not used; the partnership takes a carryover basis, and recapture is deferred, not recognized. Wrong (C): Recapture potential is preserved, not eliminated, to prevent taxpayers from avoiding recapture through contributions. Wrong (D): Recapture does not reduce the contributed property's basis.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: easy / L2 — Effect of tax credits on S corp shareholder basis
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$Archer is the sole shareholder of Voss Inc., an S corporation. Voss Inc. claims a $10,000 low-income housing tax credit and passes it through to Archer. How does the tax credit affect Archer's stock basis?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Stock basis increases by $10,000", "Stock basis decreases by $10,000", "Stock basis is not affected by tax credits", "Stock basis increases by the after-tax benefit of the credit"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 1367, tax credits passed through from an S corporation do not adjust the shareholder's stock basis. Credits reduce the shareholder's tax liability directly but are not treated as income or deduction items for basis purposes. Only income, loss, deduction, and distribution items adjust basis under the ordering rules. Wrong (A): Credits increase neither income nor basis; they directly reduce tax liability. Wrong (B): Credits do not reduce basis; only deductions and losses reduce basis under Section 1367. Wrong (D): There is no "after-tax benefit" basis adjustment concept for pass-through credits.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q19: hard / L4 — Mixing bowl transaction Section 704(c)(1)(B) seven-year rule
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$Canton contributed land (basis $75,000, FMV $350,000) to a partnership four years ago. The partnership now distributes the land to another partner, Vega, in a current distribution. The built-in gain at contribution was $275,000. Under Section 704(c)(1)(B), distributions of contributed property to another partner within seven years trigger gain recognition to the contributing partner. Evaluate the consequences to Canton.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Canton recognizes $275,000 of gain as if the property were sold at its FMV at the time of contribution", "Canton recognizes gain equal to the current FMV minus the current basis of the land", "No gain to Canton because the distribution is to Vega, not to Canton", "Canton recognizes gain only if the land has appreciated further since contribution"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 704(c)(1)(B), if contributed property is distributed to another partner within seven years, the contributing partner (Canton) must recognize the built-in gain that would have been allocated under Section 704(c) had the property been sold at its FMV at the time of contribution ($350,000 - $75,000 = $275,000). Canton's outside basis increases by the gain recognized. Wrong (B): The gain is measured using the FMV at the original contribution date, not the current FMV. Wrong (C): Section 704(c)(1)(B) specifically triggers gain to the contributing partner when contributed property goes to a different partner. Wrong (D): The gain equals the original built-in gain at contribution, regardless of subsequent appreciation.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q20: hard / L4 — S corp post-termination transition period (PTTP) and basis
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$Radcliffe S Corp's election terminated on March 1, becoming a C corporation. At termination, the sole shareholder Morris had a stock basis of $300,000, and the corporation's AAA was $120,000. During the post-termination transition period (PTTP), the corporation distributes $180,000 to Morris. Evaluate how the PTTP distribution affects Morris's stock basis and taxable income.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$180,000 is a fully taxable dividend because the corporation is now a C corp", "$120,000 is tax-free (reducing AAA to $0 and basis to $180,000), and $60,000 reduces basis to $120,000", "$120,000 is tax-free from AAA, $60,000 is a taxable dividend", "$180,000 is entirely tax-free as a return of basis"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 1371(e), during the PTTP, distributions are tax-free to the extent of the corporation's AAA ($120,000). The remaining $60,000 reduces stock basis (since there is no accumulated E&P assumed in this scenario). Morris's basis decreases from $300,000 by $120,000 (AAA) and $60,000 (basis return) to $120,000. Wrong (A): The PTTP provides a window where AAA distributions retain S corporation treatment despite the C corp conversion. Wrong (C): Without accumulated E&P, the excess distribution reduces basis rather than being treated as a dividend. Wrong (D): Only AAA distributions are explicitly tax-free under the PTTP rules; the remainder follows normal distribution ordering.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q21: hard / L3 — Partner's basis when 704(c)(1)(C) applies to contributed built-in loss property
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$Emerson contributes property with a basis of $400,000 and FMV of $150,000 to a partnership. Under Section 704(c)(1)(C), enacted in 2004, the partnership must reduce the basis of the contributed property to FMV for purposes of allocating loss to non-contributing partners. Emerson is the only contributing partner. The partnership later sells the property for $100,000. Determine how the $300,000 total loss ($400,000 basis minus $100,000 sale price) is allocated.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$300,000 loss allocated entirely to Emerson", "$150,000 loss to Emerson and $150,000 to other partners based on profit-sharing ratios", "$250,000 loss to Emerson (the built-in loss plus the post-contribution loss on Emerson's portion) and $50,000 to other partners", "All $300,000 allocated by profit-sharing ratios because the property is now a partnership asset"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 704(c)(1)(C) and the related regulations, when a partner contributes built-in loss property, the partnership takes a basis equal to FMV ($150,000) for purposes of allocating depreciation and loss to non-contributing partners. The entire built-in loss of $250,000 ($400,000 - $150,000) is allocated solely to Emerson. The additional $50,000 post-contribution loss ($150,000 - $100,000) is shared by profit ratios, but Emerson's share of that loss also accrues. In total, Emerson bears the full $300,000 if Emerson's profit share absorbs the post-contribution loss. Wrong (B): Equal splitting ignores Section 704(c)(1)(C)'s requirement to allocate built-in losses to the contributing partner. Wrong (C): The specific amounts depend on profit-sharing ratios for the post-contribution portion. Wrong (D): Section 704(c) overrides normal profit-sharing allocation for pre-contribution losses.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q22: medium / L2 — Partner guaranteed payment for capital and basis
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$Barrett receives a guaranteed payment for the use of capital under Section 707(c) of $24,000 per year from her partnership. Barrett is a 25% partner with a beginning outside basis of $100,000. Partnership ordinary income (after deducting the guaranteed payment) is $200,000. Barrett's share of ordinary income is $50,000 (25%). How does Barrett's total income and basis change for the year?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Total income of $74,000; basis increases to $150,000", "Total income of $50,000; basis increases to $150,000", "Total income of $74,000; basis increases to $174,000", "Total income of $74,000; basis increases to $126,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Barrett recognizes $74,000 total income: $24,000 guaranteed payment (ordinary income under Section 707(c)) plus $50,000 distributive share. For basis, only the distributive share ($50,000) increases outside basis because the guaranteed payment is already reflected in the reduced partnership income. Basis goes from $100,000 to $150,000. The $24,000 cash received reduces basis to $126,000 when actually distributed. Wrong (B): This excludes the guaranteed payment from total income; it is taxable ordinary income to Barrett. Wrong (C): This adds both the guaranteed payment and distributive share to basis, which double-counts the income effect. Wrong (D): The $126,000 would apply only after the $24,000 cash is distributed, which is a separate step.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q23: medium / L3 — Section 751(b) hot asset exchange on disproportionate distribution
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$Greer is a 1/3 partner in HLM Partners. The partnership holds unrealized receivables (basis $0, FMV $90,000) and capital assets (basis $120,000, FMV $210,000). Total FMV is $300,000. Greer's 1/3 share of the hot assets (unrealized receivables) is $30,000 FMV, but the partnership distributes only capital assets worth $100,000 to Greer. Under Section 751(b), determine the tax consequence of the disproportionate distribution.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No tax consequence because Greer received less than 1/3 of total partnership FMV", "Greer is treated as exchanging $30,000 of hot asset entitlement for $30,000 of additional capital assets, triggering ordinary income of $30,000", "Greer recognizes capital gain of $30,000 on the excess distribution", "Section 751(b) applies only to liquidating distributions, so there is no current consequence"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 751(b), when a distribution changes a partner's proportionate interest in hot assets (unrealized receivables and substantially appreciated inventory), the partner is treated as having exchanged hot assets for other property. Greer's 1/3 share of hot assets ($30,000 FMV) was replaced with capital assets, creating a deemed exchange. The $30,000 deemed exchange of unrealized receivables (basis $0) produces $30,000 of ordinary income. Wrong (A): The total value received is irrelevant; Section 751(b) focuses on the shift in hot asset proportions. Wrong (C): The gain on the deemed exchange of unrealized receivables is ordinary income, not capital gain. Wrong (D): Section 751(b) applies to both current and liquidating distributions.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q24: easy / L2 — Difference between S corp and partnership loss basis rules
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$Both Kline (an S corporation shareholder) and Marsh (a partner) have $100,000 of entity basis. Both entities have $60,000 of third-party bank debt that Kline and Marsh each personally guaranteed. Each entity allocates $140,000 of losses. Who can deduct the full $140,000 of loss under the basis limitation?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Both Kline and Marsh can deduct the full $140,000", "Only Marsh, because partnership liabilities increase outside basis", "Only Kline, because S corporation guarantees create debt basis", "Neither, because guaranteed debt does not increase basis for either"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 752, Marsh's outside basis includes the partner's share of partnership liabilities, bringing basis to $160,000 — sufficient to deduct the full $140,000. Under S corporation rules (IRC Section 1366(d)), Kline's guarantee of third-party bank debt does NOT create debt basis; only direct loans from the shareholder to the corporation create debt basis. Kline's basis remains $100,000, allowing only $100,000 of loss deduction. Wrong (A): S corporation shareholders cannot include guaranteed third-party debt in basis. Wrong (C): This reverses the rules; guarantees create basis for partners but not S corp shareholders. Wrong (D): Guaranteed debt does increase basis for partners under Section 752's economic risk of loss framework.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q25: medium / L3 — Qualified nonrecourse financing and partner's at-risk basis under Section 465
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$Wallace is a limited partner in a real estate partnership. Wallace contributed $60,000 cash and the partnership obtained a $500,000 qualified nonrecourse loan from a commercial bank secured solely by the real property. Wallace's share of the qualified nonrecourse financing under Section 752 is $125,000. The partnership allocates $200,000 of losses. Determine how much of the loss Wallace can deduct under the at-risk rules of Section 465.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$60,000, limited to Wallace's cash contribution only", "$185,000, because qualified nonrecourse financing is included in the at-risk amount for real estate activities", "$200,000, because all partnership losses flow through without limitation", "$125,000, limited to Wallace's share of the qualified nonrecourse financing"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 465(b)(6), qualified nonrecourse financing secured by real property is included in a taxpayer's at-risk amount for real estate activities. Wallace's at-risk amount is $185,000 ($60,000 cash + $125,000 share of qualified nonrecourse financing). The $200,000 loss is limited to $185,000, with $15,000 suspended. Wrong (A): The cash-only limitation ignores the special rule for qualified nonrecourse financing in real estate under Section 465(b)(6). Wrong (C): The at-risk rules cap deductible losses at the at-risk amount; not all losses flow through. Wrong (D): The at-risk amount includes both cash contributions and qualified nonrecourse financing, not just one component.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q26: medium / L2 — Partner's basis when liability allocation changes due to new partner admission
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$Merritt and Lowe are equal partners in ML Partners, each bearing 50% of $400,000 in recourse liabilities ($200,000 each). A new partner, Chen, is admitted for a 1/3 interest. After admission, each partner bears 1/3 of the $400,000 recourse liabilities. Merritt's outside basis before admission is $300,000. What is Merritt's outside basis after Chen's admission?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$300,000 — unchanged because no cash changed hands", "$233,333 — reduced by the decrease in Merritt's share of liabilities", "Depends on whether Chen contributes cash or property", "$366,667 — increased by Chen's contribution to the partnership"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 752(b), a decrease in a partner's share of partnership liabilities is treated as a deemed distribution. Merritt's share drops from $200,000 (50%) to $133,333 (1/3), a decrease of $66,667. This deemed distribution reduces Merritt's outside basis from $300,000 to $233,333. Wrong (A): Liability reallocation changes each partner's basis even without direct cash transactions. Wrong (C): Regardless of Chen's contribution form, the liability reallocation affects existing partners' bases. Wrong (D): Merritt's basis decreases, not increases, because Merritt's share of liabilities decreased.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q27: easy / L2 — Constructive liquidation test for nonrecourse liability allocation
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.752-3, how are partnership nonrecourse liabilities allocated among partners for purposes of determining outside basis?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Entirely to the general partner based on unlimited liability", "First to Section 704(c) minimum gain, then partnership minimum gain, then by profit-sharing ratios", "Equally among all partners regardless of ownership percentage", "Based on each partner's capital account balance at year-end"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.752-3(a), nonrecourse liabilities are allocated in three tiers: (1) to partners who would bear the economic burden due to Section 704(c) minimum gain on contributed property, (2) to partners based on their shares of partnership minimum gain, and (3) the remaining amount in accordance with each partner's share of partnership profits. Wrong (A): Nonrecourse liabilities are not allocated entirely to the general partner; that applies to certain recourse liabilities. Wrong (C): Equal allocation is not the method; profit-sharing ratios govern the residual tier. Wrong (D): Capital account balances determine economic ownership but are not the direct method for nonrecourse liability allocation.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q28: medium / L3 — S corp shareholder loan repaid and debt basis recapture
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$Phelps loaned $40,000 to her S corporation in Year 1. In Year 2, losses reduced her debt basis to $15,000 (a $25,000 reduction). In Year 3, the corporation repays the full $40,000 loan. By Year 3, income allocations have restored $10,000 of debt basis, bringing it to $25,000 before repayment. Determine the tax consequences of the $40,000 loan repayment to Phelps.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No gain; the repayment is a return of the original $40,000 loan", "Gain of $15,000 because the repayment exceeds the restored debt basis", "$25,000 of capital gain because the debt basis was reduced by prior losses", "Gain of $40,000 because the full repayment exceeds stock basis"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 1367(b)(2)(B) and Treas. Reg. 1.1367-2(c), when an S corporation repays a shareholder loan whose basis was reduced by prior losses, the shareholder recognizes gain to the extent the repayment exceeds the restored debt basis. Phelps's debt basis at repayment is $25,000 (original $40,000 - $25,000 loss reduction + $10,000 restoration). The $40,000 repayment exceeds the $25,000 debt basis by $15,000, producing $15,000 of gain. Wrong (A): The loan basis was reduced by losses; the full $40,000 is not the adjusted basis. Wrong (C): The gain is $15,000 ($40,000 - $25,000), not $25,000. Wrong (D): The gain is measured against debt basis, not stock basis.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — What does Section 721 provide for partnership contributions
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$Under IRC Section 721, what is the general rule when a partner contributes property to a partnership in exchange for a partnership interest?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No gain or loss is recognized by the partner or the partnership", "Gain is recognized to the extent FMV exceeds basis", "The partnership recognizes income equal to the FMV of property received", "Gain is deferred until the partnership sells the contributed property"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): IRC Section 721(a) provides that no gain or loss is recognized by either the partner or the partnership when property is contributed in exchange for a partnership interest. The partner takes a carryover basis under Section 722, and the partnership takes a carryover basis under Section 723. This nonrecognition rule facilitates partnership formation. Wrong (B): Section 721 defers gain recognition entirely; it is not a partial recognition rule. Wrong (C): The partnership recognizes no income on the receipt of contributed property. Wrong (D): The gain is not merely deferred by Section 721; it is preserved through the carryover basis mechanism, but no gain is recognized at contribution.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: hard / L4 — Section 754 election revocation and its consequences
(6, 'Advanced Basis Calculations', $EXPL$Ridgeway Partners made a Section 754 election eight years ago. The partnership now wants to revoke the election because recent transfers have resulted in negative basis adjustments. The partnership applies to the IRS for revocation, which is granted effective for the current year. A new partner, Yates, purchases a 20% interest for $600,000 when the partnership's total inside basis is $2,500,000 (Yates's 20% share = $500,000). Evaluate the basis consequences for Yates after the revocation.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Yates receives a $100,000 step-up because the revocation is not retroactive", "Yates receives no Section 743(b) adjustment because the 754 election has been revoked", "Yates may independently elect under Section 732(d) to obtain an adjustment on future distributions", "Both B and C — no 743(b) adjustment, but Section 732(d) is available for distributions"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Once a Section 754 election is revoked with IRS consent under Treas. Reg. 1.754-1(c), no Section 743(b) adjustment is available for Yates's purchase — the $100,000 disparity between purchase price ($600,000) and share of inside basis ($500,000) is unaddressed. However, under Section 732(d), Yates may still elect upon receiving a distribution to treat the distributed property as if a 743(b) adjustment had been in effect, partially mitigating the mismatch. Wrong (A): After revocation, no 743(b) adjustment is made; the $100,000 step-up does not occur. Wrong (B): While correct about no 743(b) adjustment, this omits the Section 732(d) alternative. Wrong (C): While correct about Section 732(d), this omits confirmation that no 743(b) adjustment applies.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4)
;

COMMIT;
